<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anthony Eden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Prime Minister Eden, Eden's</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anthony Eden was a British politician who served as Foreign Secretary and then as Prime Minister from 1955 to 1957. In 1956 he ordered military intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Israel after Egypt nationalised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a> ended in humiliating withdrawal under American pressure and is seen as a symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>'s decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anthony-eden.jpg" alt="Anthony Eden" width="307" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Walter Stoneman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anthony_Eden_(retouched)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert Anthony Eden was born on 12 June 1897 at Windlestone Hall, County Durham, the third of four sons of Sir William Eden and Sybil Frances Grey, into a conservative family of landed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>. He attended Sandroyd School in Surrey and, from January 1911, Eton College. His elder brother John was killed in action on 17 October 1914, aged 26. After the First World War Eden studied Oriental Languages, with Persian as his main language and Arabic as his second, at Christ Church, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, from October 1919. He learned French and German on continental holidays, and in February 1934 conversed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> in German.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Eden contested Spennymoor as a Conservative in 1922 without success and later sat for Warwick. In August 1931 he held his first ministerial office as Under-Secretary for Foreign Affairs. He became Foreign Secretary at 38, after Samuel Hoare resigned over the collapse of the Hoare-Laval Pact, and supported non-interference in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, but he resigned over Neville Chamberlain's policy of appeasement towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>'s Italy. In 1939 he returned to government as Secretary of State for Dominion Affairs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a>, on becoming prime minister in 1940, appointed him Secretary of State for War. He again served as Foreign Secretary from 1951, having been regarded as Churchill's crown prince since 1942.</p><p>Eden succeeded Churchill as prime minister in April 1955. In July 1956 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a> nationalised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> after Anglo-American funding for the Aswan Dam was withdrawn. Eden believed this violated the Anglo-Egyptian treaty of October 1954 and feared that Nasser meant to form an Arab alliance able to cut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s oil supplies; with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> he decided Nasser should be removed from power. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> refused to support the Anglo-French military response. Two months after ordering an end to the operation Eden resigned, on grounds of ill health and because he was widely suspected of having misled the House of Commons about collusion with France and Israel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Eden left the Commons after 33 years and entered the House of Lords as the Earl of Avon in 1961. He died of metastatic prostate cancer at Alvediston Manor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a>, on 14 January 1977, aged 79. He was twice married; his son Simon was killed in action with the RAF in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> in 1945.</p><p>He is generally considered among the least successful British prime ministers of the 20th century, though two broadly sympathetic biographies have shifted opinion somewhat, and most historians argue that he made a series of blunders at Suez, especially in not realising the depth of American opposition. Contemporaries judged him differently: Dick Crossman called him the idealist without conviction, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a> said that until Suez Eden had been in the top world class.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anthony Eden” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>President Eisenhower's administration refused support for sterling and blocked British access to IMF funds unless Britain withdrew from Egypt, and Prime Minister <b>Anthony Eden</b>'s climbdown showed that Britain could no longer pursue an independent great-power policy against American wishes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The British Foreign Minister <b>Anthony Eden</b> called for Arab unity in the 1940s, followed by proposals from pro-British leaders such as Abdullah and Nuri al-Said of Iraq, but the Egyptian proposals for a broader grouping of independent Arab states prevailed.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-arabism">Pan-Arabism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Aged 80 and in failing health, Winston Churchill stepped down as Prime Minister and was succeeded by <b>Anthony Eden</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-5">5 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Durham, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Alvediston Manor, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.78,-1.57&amp;year=1949" aria-label="Durham on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4516 289 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anthony Eden"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4966 559h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4966 559h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.78,-1.57&amp;year=1949">What was here in 1949? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
